#~
A method too large for a conditional branch to cross is still run correctly.

Big() below is one method of 30000 array reads (5000 identical lines of
6), with one more read at its start and one at its end. Every read has a
Nil guard and a bounds guard, and a guard's fault path is at the END of the
compiled method: the shared handlers in the epilogue and, for a bounds guard,
its own stub after it.

On ARM64 a conditional branch reaches a signed 19-bit word displacement, 1 MiB
of code either way, and this method compiles to more than that. The JIT
masked each displacement to 19 bits without checking it, so the first guard's
branch to its handler wrapped: the out-of-bounds read at the start of Big()
went to some other place in the method, or before its start, instead of
reporting the error. A method that long is now left to the interpreter on ARM64
(JitArm64: MAX_CODE_WORDS). AMD64 branches reach 2 GiB and compile it.

Checked here, by the default JIT, OBJECK_JIT_THRESHOLD=1 and --jit=off alike:
the sum is right over 200 calls (the method is compiled where it can be), then
an out-of-bounds index at the first read and at the last read are each
recovered as an IndexError, and a Nil array as a NilError.

The middle of Big() is generated: 5000 copies of one line.
~#
